// std/plugin.jtr — calling code you did not compile, and surviving it.
//
// TIER: `sys`. The server mode composes `std/sysproc` (a child beside its parent, with
// pipes), `std/sysstdio` (the plugin's own streams) and `std/time` (the budget); the
// one-shot mode composes `std/process`, `std/file` and `std/fs`. The frame uses `std/alog`'s
// checksum. It binds nothing itself.
//
// ─── What a plugin protocol is FOR ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A plugin runs in another process, and the entire reason for that is ISOLATION: a plugin that
// crashes, hangs on a null pointer, or corrupts its heap must not take the host with it. An
// in-process plugin is a dynamic library and shares the host's fate.
//
// So the value of this module is not the calling — it is the FAILURE TAXONOMY. Things can go
// wrong in several different ways and they have different fixes, which is why they are
// different answers rather than one `bool`:
//
//   PLUGIN_OK            the plugin answered
//   PLUGIN_FAILED        it ran and reported an error itself (its own code)
//   PLUGIN_CRASHED       it did not exit normally, or could not be started at all
//   PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE  what it wrote is not a valid response, or it wrote nothing
//   PLUGIN_REFUSED       the capability said no; nothing ran
//   PLUGIN_TIMED_OUT     it did not answer within the budget and was killed
//   PLUGIN_DOWN          the connection is over — a previous call killed it, or `hangup` did
//
// Collapsing those is what makes plugin systems miserable to debug. `PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE` in
// particular is the one everybody forgets: a plugin that exits 0 having written nothing, or
// half a message, or a message for a protocol version it invented, has FAILED — and an exit
// code of 0 says it succeeded.
//
// ─── The transport: a SERVER on a pipe, and the one-shot mode it replaces ─────
//
// **The primary API is a long-lived plugin: one process, many requests.** `connect` starts the
// plugin with `sysproc.start_piped` and keeps it; `call` writes one framed request to its
// stdin and reads one framed response from its stdout; `hangup` closes its input, drains,
// and waits. On the plugin's side `serve` is the loop a plugin's `main` runs: read a request,
// call the handler, write the response, until EOF. The cost of a process start is paid once
// per connection rather than once per call, and — the part that matters more — the host now
// has a moment in which to change its mind: a plugin that does not answer inside the budget
// is killed and the call says so. Under `system()` there was no such moment, and the old
// header recorded "no timeouts" as a genuine hole. `std/sysproc` is what closed it.
//
// **The one-shot mode is kept** — `call_once` with two file paths, `read_request`/
// `write_response` on the plugin's side — because it is a different trade, not an older
// version of the same one: a plugin invoked once per event the way a `git` hook or a linter
// is, written against `std/file` alone, with no server loop to get wrong. The two share the
// frame, the checksum and the outcome names, so a host can move a plugin from one to the
// other without re-learning what its answers mean. (It was `call`; the plain name went to
// the primary API, and Jestyr has no overloading.)
//
// ─── Framing over the pipe: length-prefixed BINARY, on raw handles ────────────
//
// The frame below is written to the pipe exactly as it is written to a file: header,
// payload, checksum trailer, no escaping. That is a deliberate choice against the recorded
// trap (A4): on Windows the C runtime's `stdout` is in TEXT mode and turns every `\n` into
// `\r\n`, which corrupts any binary frame that goes through `print_*`. The two ways out are
// a text-safe framing — newline-delimited, payload hex-encoded, which doubles every byte
// and still needs a raw stdin reader that did not exist — or bytes that never pass through
// the C runtime's text layer at all. `std/sysstdio` is the second: it reads and writes the OS
// handles directly (`ReadFile`/`WriteFile` on Windows, `read`/`write` on POSIX), and those
// have no text mode to switch. Nothing is escaped because nothing needs to be, and the
// deciding property is TESTED rather than assumed: `jplugin` sends a payload holding `\r`,
// `\n`, a NUL and bytes that are not UTF-8 through a real plugin and gets it back byte for
// byte. Payloads are `[]u8` throughout, never `String` — `from_utf8` traps on the bytes this
// protocol must carry.
//
// **A plugin serving frames must not print to stdout.** `print_str` and `sysstdio` are two
// doors onto one descriptor, and the C runtime's buffering decides the order. `eprint_str`
// is fine: stderr is a different descriptor in the plugin even when the host has merged the
// two into one pipe — in which case the diagnostic lands in the frame stream and the host
// reports `PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE`, which is the honest answer to a plugin that talked out of
// turn.
//
// ─── The budget, and what a timeout really kills ──────────────────────────────
//
// A call's budget is spent on a `time.Clock` the host was given, so a test can drive it with
// `time.manual()` and a hung plugin costs no real seconds. The host never blocks in a read it
// might not return from: it asks `sysproc.output_ready` first, sleeps a slice of the budget
// when the answer is no, and when the budget is gone it terminates the child, reaps it and
// reports `PLUGIN_TIMED_OUT`. The connection is then DOWN — a plugin that stopped answering
// mid-stream cannot be resynchronised — and the host reconnects if it wants more.
//
// **The budget is `set_budget`-able for one reason, and it was measured rather than
// guessed.** The FIRST call on a connection pays for the child's start — 75–120ms through
// `cmd.exe` here, with a 485ms outlier over 120 starts — while every call after it is a warm
// round trip on a pipe and answers in under a millisecond. One number cannot be both a
// tolerance for the first and a timeout for the rest: sized for the start it never fires,
// and sized for the round trip it fires on healthy plugins. So a host connects with a roomy
// budget, hears from the plugin once, and tightens. A timeout that trips only after the
// plugin has been heard from is evidence about the plugin; one that can trip on a cold start
// is evidence about the machine.
//
// What `terminate` reaches is the child `start_piped` started, and on Windows that is
// `cmd.exe`, not the plugin under it: the shell does not `exec`. The plugin is orphaned
// with its stdin closed; a well-behaved server exits at that EOF, a wedged one lives until
// it is not wedged. Killing the whole tree needs a process group or a Job object, which
// `sysproc` does not have (its header says so) — recorded here rather than pretended.
//
// The budget bounds READS. A request larger than the pipe's buffer (~64KB on Windows, 4KB on
// Linux) sent to a plugin that is not reading blocks in `write_all` with no budget at all;
// `PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD` is the frame's limit, not the pipe's. Bounding the write needs a
// writable-readiness query that `sysproc` does not offer.
//
// ─── The message frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//     offset 0      (4 bytes)  magic   "JPL1", so a stream that is not a message is refused
//                                      before its length is believed
//     offset 4      (2 bytes)  version protocol version, LITTLE-ENDIAN
//     offset 6      (2 bytes)  kind    request, response, or error
//     offset 8      (4 bytes)  len     payload length, LITTLE-ENDIAN
//     offset 12     (len)      payload
//     offset 12+len (4 bytes)  crc     CRC-32 of everything before it
//
// **The checksum is a TRAILER rather than a header field, and that is not cosmetic.** With it
// in the middle, the checksummed region is everything except itself — two disjoint ranges — so
// computing it needs a scratch copy, and `encode` could not be `@no_alloc`. Putting it last
// makes the region `0 .. 12+len`, one contiguous slice, and both sides check it without
// allocating anything. The `@no_alloc` contract is what surfaced this: the first version
// carried the attribute and the compiler refused it, which is the attribute doing its job.
//
// **The magic is first and is checked first**, because everything after it is only meaningful
// if this is a message at all. Without it, a truncated file of zeroes parses as a
// version-0 message of length 0 and passes its checksum. On a STREAM it does one more job:
// the header is read before the payload, so the length is believed only once the magic is
// right, and a plugin that wrote prose instead of a frame is refused after twelve bytes
// rather than after a four-gigabyte read.
//
// **An ERROR frame is how a server-mode plugin fails without dying.** In one-shot mode a
// plugin reports an error with its exit code; a server has no exit code to give per call, so
// it answers with `PLUGIN_KIND_ERROR` — the first four bytes of the payload its own code,
// little-endian, the rest a message — and stays alive. The host reports `PLUGIN_FAILED`
// with that code in `exit_code` and the message in the response buffer, so the same field
// means "the plugin's own code" in both modes.
//
// **The checksum is `alog.crc32`**, deliberately reused rather than reimplemented: two CRC
// implementations in one tree is two places to be wrong about one question, and that one is
// already pinned against the published vector.
//
// **Byte order is written explicitly.** A plugin may be compiled by a different toolchain on a
// different machine — that is the entire point of a plugin — so the frame cannot inherit the
// host's order. The same rule `std/alog` and `std/sysnet` follow.
//
// ─── Version negotiation, and why it is a REFUSAL and not a fallback ──────────
//
// The request carries `PLUGIN_VERSION`. A plugin that does not know that version must refuse —
// `read_request` returns `PLUGIN_BAD_VERSION`, `serve` exits with `PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_VERSION` —
// rather than answer.
//
// It is not a negotiation, and that is a decision: a plugin that "does its best" with a
// version it does not understand produces answers the host cannot interpret and cannot
// distinguish from correct ones. A clean refusal is a bug report; a best effort is a mystery.
//
// ─── What this does NOT do ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// No concurrent calls on one connection (a call is a request then its response, in turn), no
// plugin-initiated messages, no streaming payloads, and no sandbox: a plugin is a program
// running with the host's privileges. `sysproc.denied()` and `process.denied()` refuse to run
// anything at all, which is the only enforcement this tier has. `serve` reads stdin and
// nothing else, so a plugin cannot be a server AND take arguments for its work — the
// arguments it was started with are the configuration it gets.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/plugin_server_demo.jtr (host) + examples/std/plugin_echo.jtr
//                  (plugin, both modes); examples/std/plugin_demo.jtr (the one-shot host)
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/plugin_test.jtr

import "alog"
import "file"
import "fs"
import "process"
import "str"
import "sysproc"
import "sysstdio"
import "time"

// ---- the frame ------------------------------------------------------------------

pub const PLUGIN_HEADER: usize = 12
pub const PLUGIN_TRAILER: usize = 4
pub const PLUGIN_VERSION: i64 = 1
pub const PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD: usize = 4194304

pub const PLUGIN_KIND_REQUEST: i64 = 1
pub const PLUGIN_KIND_RESPONSE: i64 = 2
pub const PLUGIN_KIND_ERROR: i64 = 3       // a server-mode plugin's own failure, with its code

// ---- what a call can answer ------------------------------------------------------
//
// Seven, because there are seven different fixes. See the header.

pub const PLUGIN_OK: i32 = 0
pub const PLUGIN_FAILED: i32 = 1           // it ran and reported an error itself
pub const PLUGIN_CRASHED: i32 = 2          // did not exit normally, or never started
pub const PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE: i32 = 3     // wrote something unusable, or nothing
pub const PLUGIN_REFUSED: i32 = 4          // the capability said no
pub const PLUGIN_TIMED_OUT: i32 = 5        // did not answer in the budget; killed
pub const PLUGIN_DOWN: i32 = 6             // the connection is over; reconnect

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn outcome_name(o: i32) -> str {
    if o == PLUGIN_OK { return "ok" }
    if o == PLUGIN_FAILED { return "failed" }
    if o == PLUGIN_CRASHED { return "crashed" }
    if o == PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE { return "bad-response" }
    if o == PLUGIN_REFUSED { return "refused" }
    if o == PLUGIN_TIMED_OUT { return "timed-out" }
    if o == PLUGIN_DOWN { return "down" }
    return "unknown"
}

// What a plugin's `read_request` reports.
pub const PLUGIN_REQ_OK: i32 = 0
pub const PLUGIN_BAD_FRAME: i32 = 1        // not a message, or corrupt
pub const PLUGIN_BAD_VERSION: i32 = 2      // a version this plugin does not know

// What `serve` returns, for the plugin's `main` to return. Distinct numbers so a host — or a
// person reading a log — can tell which refusal ended the server without a response to read.
pub const PLUGIN_EXIT_OK: i32 = 0          // the host hung up between frames
pub const PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_FRAME: i32 = 3   // the stream is not frames
pub const PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_VERSION: i32 = 4 // a version this plugin does not know
pub const PLUGIN_EXIT_WRITE_FAILED: i32 = 6 // the host went away mid-answer
pub const PLUGIN_EXIT_TRUNCATED: i32 = 7   // EOF inside a frame

// How long the host sleeps between readiness checks while a call's budget runs. A floor,
// not a promise — `time.wait` reports what it really waited, and that is what is charged.
const PLUGIN_POLL_NANOS: i64 = 1000000

// ---- little-endian, written explicitly -------------------------------------------

@no_alloc @no_os fn put_u16(mut b: []u8, at: usize, v: i64) {
    b[at + 0] = (v & 255) as u8
    b[at + 1] = ((v >> 8) & 255) as u8
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn put_u32(mut b: []u8, at: usize, v: i64) {
    b[at + 0] = (v & 255) as u8
    b[at + 1] = ((v >> 8) & 255) as u8
    b[at + 2] = ((v >> 16) & 255) as u8
    b[at + 3] = ((v >> 24) & 255) as u8
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn get_u16(read b: []u8, at: usize) -> i64 {
    return (b[at + 0] as i64) | ((b[at + 1] as i64) << 8)
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn get_u32(read b: []u8, at: usize) -> i64 {
    return (b[at + 0] as i64) | ((b[at + 1] as i64) << 8) | ((b[at + 2] as i64) << 16) | ((b[at + 3] as i64) << 24)
}

// `JPL1`. Checked before anything else in the frame is believed.
@no_alloc @no_os fn magic_ok(read b: []u8) -> bool {
    if b.len < 4 { return false }
    if (b[0] as i32) != 74 { return false }        // J
    if (b[1] as i32) != 80 { return false }        // P
    if (b[2] as i32) != 76 { return false }        // L
    if (b[3] as i32) != 49 { return false }        // 1
    return true
}

// Encode a message into `out`. Returns its total length, or 0 if `out` is too small.
//
// **Public, and pure**, so both sides of the protocol use the same encoder and a test can
// build a message without a plugin. A host and a plugin that encoded separately would be two
// implementations of one format, which is the shape `std/alog`'s test deliberately takes ON
// PURPOSE for cross-checking — and which a shipped protocol must never have.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn encode(mut dst: []u8, kind: i64, version: i64, read payload: []u8) -> usize {
    let total: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + payload.len
    if dst.len < total { return 0 }
    if payload.len > PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD { return 0 }
    dst[0] = 74 as u8
    dst[1] = 80 as u8
    dst[2] = 76 as u8
    dst[3] = 49 as u8
    put_u16(dst, 4, version)
    put_u16(dst, 6, kind)
    put_u32(dst, 8, payload.len as i64)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < payload.len {
        dst[PLUGIN_HEADER + i] = payload[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    // The checksum is the TRAILER, over everything before it — one contiguous slice, which is
    // what lets this function be `@no_alloc`. See the header.
    put_u32(dst, PLUGIN_HEADER + payload.len, alog.crc32(dst[0 .. PLUGIN_HEADER + payload.len]) as i64)
    return total
}

// **The header, read on its own.** On a stream the host and the plugin both read twelve
// bytes, ask this, and only then read the payload it announces. Returns the payload length,
// or -1 for anything that is not the header of a message: bad magic, an impossible length.
// The kind and the version are NOT judged here — a whole frame is needed for the checksum,
// and the checksum is checked before either is trusted (see `decode`).
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn header_len(read hdr: []u8) -> isize {
    if hdr.len < PLUGIN_HEADER { return 0 - 1 }
    if magic_ok(hdr) == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let plen: i64 = get_u32(hdr, 8)
    if plen < 0 or plen > (PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD as i64) { return 0 - 1 }
    return plen as isize
}

// The kind a whole frame claims. Meaningful only after `decode_any` accepted it.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn frame_kind(read frame: []u8) -> i64 {
    if frame.len < PLUGIN_HEADER { return 0 }
    return get_u16(frame, 6)
}

// Validate a frame and report its payload length, or -1 if it is not a usable message of
// `want_kind`. `bad_version` is set when the frame is well-formed but speaks another version —
// the one failure a caller reports differently from corruption.
@no_alloc @no_os fn decode(read frame: []u8, want_kind: i64, mut bad_version: bool) -> isize {
    let plen: isize = decode_any(frame, bad_version)
    if plen < 0 { return plen }
    if get_u16(frame, 6) != want_kind { return 0 - 1 }
    return plen
}

// `decode` without the kind check — a server-mode host accepts EITHER a response or an
// error frame and asks `frame_kind` afterwards.
@no_alloc @no_os fn decode_any(read frame: []u8, mut bad_version: bool) -> isize {
    bad_version = false
    if frame.len < PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER { return 0 - 1 }
    if magic_ok(frame) == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let plen: i64 = get_u32(frame, 8)
    if plen < 0 or plen > (PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD as i64) { return 0 - 1 }
    if ((PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER) as i64) + plen != (frame.len as i64) { return 0 - 1 }
    // **The checksum is verified BEFORE the version is trusted.** A corrupt frame can carry any
    // version at all, and reporting "unsupported version" for what is really bit rot sends a
    // caller after entirely the wrong bug.
    let body: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + (plen as usize)
    if (alog.crc32(frame[0 .. body]) as i64) != get_u32(frame, body) { return 0 - 1 }
    if get_u16(frame, 4) != PLUGIN_VERSION {
        bad_version = true
        return 0 - 1
    }
    return plen as isize
}

// ---- what a call answers ---------------------------------------------------------

// What a call answers: an outcome, the plugin's own code, and the response length.
//
// `@copy`: three scalars. The response BYTES land in the caller's buffer, which is the shape
// every reader in this tree takes — a `Reply` that owned its payload would be an allocation
// per call for data the caller already has somewhere to put.
//
// `exit_code` is the plugin's OWN code: its exit code in one-shot mode, the code it put in an
// error frame in server mode, the signal or exit code that ended it for `PLUGIN_CRASHED`.
// For `PLUGIN_FAILED` from a server, `len` is the length of the plugin's message, copied into
// the response buffer — a failure that can describe itself should be allowed to.
@copy pub struct Reply {
    pub outcome: i32,
    pub exit_code: i32,
    pub len: usize,
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn succeeded(read r: Reply) -> bool { return r.outcome == PLUGIN_OK }

// ---- the host side: SERVER MODE ------------------------------------------------------

// A connection to a running plugin. `@move` because it owns a `sysproc.Child`, which is
// itself a resource the language refuses to duplicate; `hangup` exactly once, and `is_up`
// says whether that has happened (or a failure did it first).
@move pub struct Conn {
    c: sysproc.Child,
    clk: time.Clock,
    budget: i64,
    calls: i64,
    failures: i64,
    up: bool,
}

// A `Conn` that names nothing: what `connect` hands back through `catch`, and what a caller
// compares against without a separate optional. Every call on it is `PLUGIN_DOWN`.
pub fn no_conn() -> Conn {
    return Conn{ c: sysproc.no_child(), clk: time.manual(0), budget: 0, calls: 0, failures: 0, up: false }
}

// Start `cmd` as a plugin server and keep it. `budget_nanos` is what each call may take
// before the plugin is killed; `clk` is where that time is measured, so a test hands in
// `time.manual()` and a hung plugin costs nothing real.
//
// The same command semantics as `sysproc.start_piped` — the platform shell — and the same
// capability gate, refused as `PluginRefused` before anything runs.
pub fn connect(mut sp: sysproc.Spawner, take clk: time.Clock, read cmd: str, budget_nanos: i64) -> Conn !{ PluginRefused, PluginNoStart(i32) } {
    if sysproc.can_start(sp) == false {
        // Through the spawner, so its refusal counter sees the attempt the way it would
        // have seen a direct call — a test that hands code a denied spawner asks both.
        var none: sysproc.Child = sysproc.start_piped(sp, cmd) catch sysproc.no_child()
        return err(PluginRefused)
    }
    var code: i32 = 0
    var ch: sysproc.Child = sysproc.start_piped(sp, cmd) catch |e| match e {
        SysProcFailed(n) => note_start_failure(n, code),
        _ => sysproc.no_child()
    }
    if sysproc.is_live(ch) == false { return err(PluginNoStart(code)) }
    return ok(Conn{ c: ch, clk: clk, budget: budget_nanos, calls: 0, failures: 0, up: true })
}

fn note_start_failure(n: i32, mut code: i32) -> sysproc.Child {
    code = n
    return sysproc.no_child()
}

// Is the plugin still there to be called?
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_up(read h: Conn) -> bool { return h.up }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn conn_calls(read h: Conn) -> i64 { return h.calls }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn conn_failures(read h: Conn) -> i64 { return h.failures }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn budget_of(read h: Conn) -> i64 { return h.budget }

// Change what the NEXT call may take. The budget belongs to the connection rather than to
// the call because it is also what bounds `hangup`'s drain, but a host does not owe every
// call the same patience: a handshake on a cold process must tolerate the tens or hundreds
// of milliseconds a process start costs, while the call after it is a warm round trip on a
// pipe and a host that still allowed seconds for it has no timeout worth the name.
//
// Splitting those is what makes a timeout a PROPERTY instead of a race. A budget that must
// cover a cold start is necessarily far larger than a warm answer needs, so a call that
// exceeds it proves something only when the process is known to be up — and the way to know
// that is to have just heard from it, then tighten. `plugin_server_demo` does exactly that,
// and it is why its transcript is pinnable at all.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn set_budget(mut h: Conn, nanos: i64) { h.budget = nanos }

// How the plugin ended, once it has: `SPROC_RUNNING` while the connection is up. The
// child's cached status, which `sysproc` keeps after `release` for exactly this question.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn exit_status(read h: Conn) -> sysproc.Status { return sysproc.last_status(h.c) }

// What a bounded read reports.
const PLUGIN_RD_OK: i32 = 0
const PLUGIN_RD_TIMEOUT: i32 = 1
const PLUGIN_RD_EOF: i32 = 2
const PLUGIN_RD_ERROR: i32 = 3

// Read exactly `buf.len` bytes of the plugin's output, or say why not. Never blocks in a
// read: readiness is asked first, and `waited` — carried across the reads of one call —
// is charged with what the clock really slept.
fn read_exact(mut h: Conn, mut buf: []u8, mut waited: i64) -> i32 {
    var got: usize = 0
    for got < buf.len {
        if sysproc.output_ready(h.c) {
            var win: []u8 = buf[got .. buf.len]
            let n: i64 = sysproc.read_output(h.c, win)
            if n == 0 { return PLUGIN_RD_EOF }
            if n < 0 { return PLUGIN_RD_ERROR }
            got = got + n as usize
        } else {
            if waited >= h.budget { return PLUGIN_RD_TIMEOUT }
            var slice: i64 = PLUGIN_POLL_NANOS
            let left: i64 = h.budget - waited
            if slice > left { slice = left }
            waited = waited + time.wait(h.clk, slice)
        }
    }
    return PLUGIN_RD_OK
}

// The plugin stopped talking mid-call: find out how it ended and name it. EOF with a clean
// exit is `PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE` (it promised an answer and gave none — the one-shot rule); a
// non-zero exit is `PLUGIN_FAILED` with that code; a signal, or on Windows an NTSTATUS exit
// (the high bit set, so negative as `i32` — what an access violation reports), is
// `PLUGIN_CRASHED`. Bounded: a plugin whose shell lingers is killed after the budget.
fn went_away(mut h: Conn) -> Reply {
    let st: sysproc.Status = sysproc.wait_or_kill(h.c, h.clk, h.budget)
    h.up = false
    let code: i32 = sysproc.code_of(st)
    if sysproc.is_signalled(st) { return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_CRASHED, code, 0) }
    if code < 0 { return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_CRASHED, code, 0) }
    if code != 0 { return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_FAILED, code, 0) }
    return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, 0, 0)
}

// The budget is gone: kill it, reap it, and say so. `wait_or_kill` with no budget is one
// liveness check, the kill, the reap and the release — the composition every timeout wants.
fn give_up(mut h: Conn) -> Reply {
    let st: sysproc.Status = sysproc.wait_or_kill(h.c, h.clk, 0)
    h.up = false
    return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_TIMED_OUT, sysproc.code_of(st), 0)
}

// The stream cannot be trusted any more (bad magic, a failed checksum): the plugin is
// coherent enough to be hung up on politely, so it is — and the connection is over.
fn desync(mut h: Conn) -> Reply {
    let st: sysproc.Status = end_conn(h)
    return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, sysproc.code_of(st), 0)
}

fn fail_conn(mut h: Conn, outcome: i32, code: i32, len: usize) -> Reply {
    h.failures = h.failures + 1
    return Reply{ outcome: outcome, exit_code: code, len: len }
}

// Send one request and read its answer. The response bytes land in `response`; `len` says
// how many. A plugin that answers with an error frame is `PLUGIN_FAILED` and stays up; every
// other failure ends the connection, and `is_up` says so.
pub fn call(mut h: Conn, read request: []u8, mut response: []u8) -> Reply {
    h.calls = h.calls + 1
    if h.up == false { return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_DOWN, sysproc.code_of(sysproc.last_status(h.c)), 0) }

    // The request, as one write. See the header on what bounds this and what does not.
    let total: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + request.len
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, total)
    var frame: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, total)
    let n: usize = encode(frame, PLUGIN_KIND_REQUEST, PLUGIN_VERSION, request)
    if n == 0 {
        free_ptr(raw)
        return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0, 0)
    }
    let put: i64 = sysproc.write_all(h.c, frame[0 .. n])
    free_ptr(raw)
    // A short write means the reader is gone — the plugin died before or while reading.
    if put != (n as i64) { return went_away(h) }

    // The header first, so the length is believed only once the magic is right.
    var waited: i64 = 0
    var hraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, PLUGIN_HEADER)
    var hdr: []u8 = slice(u8, hraw, PLUGIN_HEADER)
    let hr: i32 = read_exact(h, hdr, waited)
    if hr != PLUGIN_RD_OK {
        free_ptr(hraw)
        if hr == PLUGIN_RD_TIMEOUT { return give_up(h) }
        return went_away(h)
    }
    let plen: isize = header_len(hdr)
    if plen < 0 {
        free_ptr(hraw)
        return desync(h)
    }

    // Then the whole frame, header copied in front so the checksum covers what it must.
    let flen: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + (plen as usize)
    var fraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, flen)
    var whole: []u8 = slice(u8, fraw, flen)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < PLUGIN_HEADER {
        whole[i] = hdr[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    free_ptr(hraw)
    var rest: []u8 = whole[PLUGIN_HEADER .. flen]
    let rr: i32 = read_exact(h, rest, waited)
    if rr != PLUGIN_RD_OK {
        free_ptr(fraw)
        if rr == PLUGIN_RD_TIMEOUT { return give_up(h) }
        return went_away(h)
    }
    var badver: bool = false
    let got: isize = decode_any(whole, badver)
    if got < 0 {
        free_ptr(fraw)
        return desync(h)
    }
    let kind: i64 = frame_kind(whole)

    // An error frame: the plugin's own code, then its message, and the plugin stays up.
    if kind == PLUGIN_KIND_ERROR {
        if got < 4 {
            free_ptr(fraw)
            return desync(h)
        }
        let code: i32 = get_u32(whole, PLUGIN_HEADER) as i32
        let mlen: usize = (got as usize) - 4
        var take_len: usize = mlen
        if take_len > response.len { take_len = response.len }
        var k: usize = 0
        for k < take_len {
            response[k] = whole[PLUGIN_HEADER + 4 + k]
            k = k + 1
        }
        free_ptr(fraw)
        return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_FAILED, code, take_len)
    }
    if kind != PLUGIN_KIND_RESPONSE {
        free_ptr(fraw)
        return desync(h)
    }
    if (got as usize) > response.len {
        free_ptr(fraw)
        // The connection is still coherent — the plugin answered, the caller's buffer is
        // what fell short — but the answer is lost, and a caller that retries with a bigger
        // buffer needs the connection, so it is kept.
        return fail_conn(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, 0, 0)
    }
    var j: usize = 0
    for j < got as usize {
        response[j] = whole[PLUGIN_HEADER + j]
        j = j + 1
    }
    free_ptr(fraw)
    return Reply{ outcome: PLUGIN_OK, exit_code: 0, len: got as usize }
}

// Close the plugin's input, drain what it still has to say, and wait for it — the one
// deadlock-free order, bounded by the budget at each step so a plugin that will not leave
// is killed rather than waited on. Idempotent: a second hangup reports the first's status.
pub fn hangup(mut h: Conn) -> sysproc.Status {
    if h.up == false { return sysproc.last_status(h.c) }
    return end_conn(h)
}

fn end_conn(mut h: Conn) -> sysproc.Status {
    sysproc.close_input(h.c)
    // Drain, bounded. A well-behaved server writes nothing after its last answer and closes
    // at EOF; this loop ends on that EOF, or on the budget.
    var waited: i64 = 0
    var scratch: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 4096)
    var win: []u8 = slice(u8, scratch, 4096)
    var going: bool = true
    for going {
        if sysproc.output_ready(h.c) {
            let n: i64 = sysproc.read_output(h.c, win)
            if n <= 0 { going = false }
        } else {
            if waited >= h.budget {
                going = false
            } else {
                var slice: i64 = PLUGIN_POLL_NANOS
                let left: i64 = h.budget - waited
                if slice > left { slice = left }
                waited = waited + time.wait(h.clk, slice)
            }
        }
    }
    free_ptr(scratch)
    let st: sysproc.Status = sysproc.wait_or_kill(h.c, h.clk, h.budget)
    h.up = false
    return st
}

// ---- the plugin side: SERVER MODE -------------------------------------------------------

// What a handler is told about the call it is answering, and what it tells back.
//
// `len` is the handler's: how many bytes of the response buffer it filled. `count` is the
// server's: how many requests this process has answered before this one, so a plugin can
// prove in its reply that it is one long-lived process rather than one per call.
pub struct Served {
    pub len: usize,
    pub count: i64,
}

// A handler answers one request. The request bytes are read-only; the response buffer is
// the handler's to fill, reporting how much in `s.len`. It returns 0 to send the buffer as
// the answer, or its own non-zero code to send an ERROR frame carrying that code and the
// buffer as a message — the host sees `PLUGIN_FAILED` with the code. The `runtime.Task`
// shape (a fn pointer plus the context it is called with), for the reason every handler in
// this tree takes it: a closure that captures cannot be stored, a fn pointer can.
pub fn serve(ctx: *mut u8, f: fn(*mut u8, read []u8, mut []u8, mut Served) -> i32, cap: usize) -> i32 {
    var hraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, PLUGIN_HEADER)
    var hdr: []u8 = slice(u8, hraw, PLUGIN_HEADER)
    // Frame buffers: the largest request or response this server will carry.
    let fcap: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + cap
    var iraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, fcap)
    var inb: []u8 = slice(u8, iraw, fcap)
    var rraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap)
    var resp: []u8 = slice(u8, rraw, cap)
    var oraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, fcap + 4)
    var outb: []u8 = slice(u8, oraw, fcap + 4)
    var mraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, cap + 4)
    var msg: []u8 = slice(u8, mraw, cap + 4)

    var count: i64 = 0
    var code: i32 = PLUGIN_EXIT_OK
    var going: bool = true
    for going {
        // The header, on its own. Nothing at all is the host hanging up — the normal end.
        let hn: usize = sysstdio.read_stdin_exact(hdr)
        if hn == 0 {
            going = false
        } else if hn < PLUGIN_HEADER {
            code = PLUGIN_EXIT_TRUNCATED
            going = false
        } else {
            let plen: isize = header_len(hdr)
            if plen < 0 or (plen as usize) > cap {
                code = PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_FRAME
                going = false
            } else {
                let flen: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + (plen as usize)
                var i: usize = 0
                for i < PLUGIN_HEADER {
                    inb[i] = hdr[i]
                    i = i + 1
                }
                var rest: []u8 = inb[PLUGIN_HEADER .. flen]
                let rn: usize = sysstdio.read_stdin_exact(rest)
                if rn < rest.len {
                    code = PLUGIN_EXIT_TRUNCATED
                    going = false
                } else {
                    var badver: bool = false
                    let got: isize = decode(inb[0 .. flen], PLUGIN_KIND_REQUEST, badver)
                    if got < 0 {
                        if badver { code = PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_VERSION } else { code = PLUGIN_EXIT_BAD_FRAME }
                        going = false
                    } else {
                        var s: Served = Served{ len: 0, count: count }
                        let rc: i32 = f(ctx, inb[PLUGIN_HEADER .. PLUGIN_HEADER + (got as usize)], resp, s)
                        var rlen: usize = s.len
                        if rlen > cap { rlen = cap }
                        var on: usize = 0
                        if rc == 0 {
                            on = encode(outb, PLUGIN_KIND_RESPONSE, PLUGIN_VERSION, resp[0 .. rlen])
                        } else {
                            // The code first, then the message — the layout the host reads.
                            put_u32(msg, 0, rc as i64)
                            var k: usize = 0
                            for k < rlen {
                                msg[4 + k] = resp[k]
                                k = k + 1
                            }
                            on = encode(outb, PLUGIN_KIND_ERROR, PLUGIN_VERSION, msg[0 .. 4 + rlen])
                        }
                        // **A failed write must NOT exit 0.** A half-written response with a
                        // success code is exactly what the host cannot detect any other way.
                        if on == 0 or sysstdio.write_stdout_all(outb[0 .. on]) != (on as i64) {
                            code = PLUGIN_EXIT_WRITE_FAILED
                            going = false
                        }
                        count = count + 1
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    free_ptr(mraw)
    free_ptr(oraw)
    free_ptr(rraw)
    free_ptr(iraw)
    free_ptr(hraw)
    return code
}

// ---- the host side: ONE-SHOT MODE -----------------------------------------------------
//
// One request, one response, one process per call: the host writes a request file, runs the
// plugin with both paths as arguments, and reads the response file. See the header on when
// this is the right shape. Nothing below changed when the server mode arrived.

// A host that may call plugins. It is `process.Process` plus `fs.Fs` because a call needs
// both — and both are the caller's to grant, so a function that calls plugins says so in its
// signature exactly as one that runs commands does.
@move pub struct Host {
    p: process.Process,
    f: fs.Fs,
    calls: i64,
    failures: i64,
}

pub fn host(take p: process.Process, take f: fs.Fs) -> Host {
    return Host{ p: p, f: f, calls: 0, failures: 0 }
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn calls(read h: Host) -> i64 { return h.calls }
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn failures(read h: Host) -> i64 { return h.failures }

// Call `cmd`, handing it `request` and reading its answer into `response`.
//
// `req_path` and `resp_path` are the two files the plugin is told about; they are the caller's
// to name so a host running several plugins can keep them apart, and so a test can look at
// them afterwards.
//
// **The command is built as `<cmd> <req_path> <resp_path>`**, which means `cmd` may carry its
// own arguments and the two paths always arrive last. A path containing a space would break
// that, and this module does NOT quote it: quoting differs between `cmd.exe` and `sh`, and a
// half-right quoting rule is worse than a documented restriction. `plugin_paths_must_not_
// contain_spaces` pins the refusal.
pub fn call_once(mut h: Host, read a: Allocator, read cmd: str, read req_path: str, read resp_path: str,
            read request: []u8, mut response: []u8) -> Reply {
    h.calls = h.calls + 1
    // **Both capabilities are checked BEFORE anything happens**, and the process one is
    // checked rather than inferred from `run`'s answer. `std/process`'s own header says why:
    // a denied handle reports -1 and so does a real `system()` failure, so -1 is deliberately
    // not a discriminator. Asking `can_run` first is what keeps `PLUGIN_REFUSED` distinct
    // from `PLUGIN_CRASHED` — and those are the two outcomes a caller most needs to tell
    // apart, since one is its own configuration and the other is the plugin's fault.
    if process.can_run(h.p) == false { return fail(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0) }
    if fs.can_write(h.f) == false { return fail(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0) }
    // `cmd` MAY contain spaces — it carries the plugin's own arguments. The two PATHS may not,
    // because they are appended to a shell command line unquoted. See the header on why this
    // refuses rather than quoting.
    if has_space(req_path) or has_space(resp_path) { return fail(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0) }

    // Write the request.
    let total: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + request.len
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, total)
    var frame: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, total)
    let n: usize = encode(frame, PLUGIN_KIND_REQUEST, PLUGIN_VERSION, request)
    if n == 0 {
        free_ptr(raw)
        return fail(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0)
    }
    var w: file.Writer = file.create_path(a, req_path)
    file.write_from(w, frame[0 .. n])
    let put: usize = file.finish(w) catch 0
    free_ptr(raw)
    if put != n { return fail(h, PLUGIN_REFUSED, 0) }

    // Remove any stale response BEFORE running, so a plugin that writes nothing cannot be
    // mistaken for one that answered — the previous call's file would otherwise be read as
    // this call's reply. That is the subtlest bug in a file-based protocol and it is silent.
    fs.erase(h.f, resp_path)

    var line: String = string_new()
    string_push(line, cmd)
    string_push(line, " ")
    string_push(line, req_path)
    string_push(line, " ")
    string_push(line, resp_path)
    let code: i32 = process.run(h.p, string_view(line))
    string_free(line)

    // `run` reports -1 for "did not exit normally" — signalled, or the shell could not start
    // it. That is the isolation this whole module exists for, so it is its own outcome.
    if code < 0 { return fail(h, PLUGIN_CRASHED, code) }
    if code != 0 { return fail(h, PLUGIN_FAILED, code) }

    // **Exit 0 is not success.** A plugin that wrote nothing, or half a message, or a message
    // for a version it invented, has failed — and its exit code says otherwise.
    var r: file.Reader = file.open_path(a, resp_path)
    if file.is_open(r) == false { return fail(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, code) }
    let size: i64 = file.size(r)
    if size < ((PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER) as i64) or size > ((PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD) as i64) {
        file.close(r)
        return fail(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, code)
    }
    var rraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, size as usize)
    var rframe: []u8 = slice(u8, rraw, size as usize)
    let got: usize = file.read_into(r, rframe)
    file.close(r)
    var badver: bool = false
    var plen: isize = 0 - 1
    if got == (size as usize) { plen = decode(rframe, PLUGIN_KIND_RESPONSE, badver) }
    if plen < 0 {
        free_ptr(rraw)
        return fail(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, code)
    }
    if (plen as usize) > response.len {
        free_ptr(rraw)
        return fail(h, PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE, code)
    }
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < (plen as usize) {
        response[i] = rframe[PLUGIN_HEADER + i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    free_ptr(rraw)
    return Reply{ outcome: PLUGIN_OK, exit_code: code, len: plen as usize }
}

fn fail(mut h: Host, outcome: i32, code: i32) -> Reply {
    h.failures = h.failures + 1
    return Reply{ outcome: outcome, exit_code: code, len: 0 }
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn has_space(read s: str) -> bool {
    for i in 0..s.len {
        if (s[i] as i32) == 32 { return true }
    }
    return false
}

// ---- the plugin side: ONE-SHOT MODE ------------------------------------------------------

// Read the request a host wrote at `path` into `payload`. Returns its length, or -1 with
// `status` saying why.
//
// A plugin's whole contract is: read this, answer, exit 0. Anything else — a bad frame, a
// version it does not know — must exit NON-ZERO, because the host reads an exit code of 0 as
// a promise that a valid response is waiting.
pub fn read_request(read a: Allocator, read path: str, mut payload: []u8, mut status: i32) -> isize {
    status = PLUGIN_BAD_FRAME
    var r: file.Reader = file.open_path(a, path)
    if file.is_open(r) == false { return 0 - 1 }
    let size: i64 = file.size(r)
    if size < ((PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER) as i64) or size > ((PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + PLUGIN_MAX_PAYLOAD) as i64) {
        file.close(r)
        return 0 - 1
    }
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, size as usize)
    var frame: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, size as usize)
    let got: usize = file.read_into(r, frame)
    file.close(r)
    var badver: bool = false
    var plen: isize = 0 - 1
    if got == (size as usize) { plen = decode(frame, PLUGIN_KIND_REQUEST, badver) }
    if plen < 0 {
        if badver { status = PLUGIN_BAD_VERSION }
        free_ptr(raw)
        return 0 - 1
    }
    if (plen as usize) > payload.len {
        free_ptr(raw)
        return 0 - 1
    }
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < (plen as usize) {
        payload[i] = frame[PLUGIN_HEADER + i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    free_ptr(raw)
    status = PLUGIN_REQ_OK
    return plen as isize
}

// Write a response to `path`. True if it went out whole.
//
// A plugin that returns false here must exit non-zero: a half-written response with an exit
// code of 0 is precisely the `PLUGIN_BAD_RESPONSE` case, and the plugin is the only side that
// knows it happened.
pub fn write_response(read a: Allocator, read path: str, read payload: []u8) -> bool {
    let total: usize = PLUGIN_HEADER + PLUGIN_TRAILER + payload.len
    var raw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, total)
    var frame: []u8 = slice(u8, raw, total)
    let n: usize = encode(frame, PLUGIN_KIND_RESPONSE, PLUGIN_VERSION, payload)
    var okay: bool = false
    if n > 0 {
        var w: file.Writer = file.create_path(a, path)
        file.write_from(w, frame[0 .. n])
        let put: usize = file.finish(w) catch 0
        if put == n { okay = true }
    }
    free_ptr(raw)
    return okay
}
